## Steps

00-context              starting state, identity, quota, plan
01-create-ocm-role      <- THE FIX; create + link ocm-role to Red Hat1
02-create-user-role     link OCM user to the AWS account
03-create-account-roles 3 HCP account roles (account-wide, reusable)
04-create-network       VPC + subnets via CloudFormation  (~3.5 min)
05-create-oidc-config   managed OIDC  (independent of 04, ran in parallel)
06-create-operator-roles 8 operator roles bound to that OIDC config
07-create-cluster       dry-run then real; ready ~10 min after create

Steps 04 and 05/06 have no dependency on each other and can run in parallel.
Steps 01-03 are account-wide and reusable -- a second cluster in this account
only needs 04-07 (and 05/06 only if you want a fresh OIDC config).

## Access

08-cluster-access. HCP gives you NO kubeadmin and NO kubeconfig file -- you
create a credential, then `oc login` writes your local ~/.kube/config.

## Teardown — DONE, see 09-teardown

The cluster and everything else created here was torn down the same day and
the account is back to its pre-2026-10-12 state (no roles, no VPC, nothing
billing). 09-teardown has the full sequence and two gotchas worth knowing:

  - delete-stack FAILS the first time. HCP leaves a
    '<cluster-id>-vpce-private-router' security group that CloudFormation
    does not own and the uninstall does not remove; it blocks the VPC
    delete. Remove it, then retry.
  - use `rosa delete oidc-config`, NOT the `rosa delete oidc-provider` that
    `rosa delete cluster` suggests -- the latter is the Classic path and
    leaves the managed config registered in OCM.

## Doing this again

rosa-hcp.sh in the repo root automates all of 01-08 and the teardown,
including both gotchas above:

  AWS_PROFILE=saml ./rosa-hcp.sh --cluster-name mytest
  AWS_PROFILE=saml ./rosa-hcp.sh --cluster-name mytest --delete

It is NOT yet exercised end to end -- it was written from these steps but
never run, since the cluster was torn down by hand first.
